# App modals

In `@stream-kit/app`, use the **built-in modal system** (`app.createModal` / `app.modals`) for dialogs and slide-over panels. Do **not** add standalone `bits-ui` `Dialog` wrappers in app components unless you are editing the modal shell itself (`$lib/components/core/modal/modal.svelte`).

## Opening a modal

```ts
import { app } from '$lib/core';
import MyForm from './my-form.svelte';
import MyFormFooter from './my-form-footer.svelte';

app.createModal({
  id: 'unique-modal-id',
  title: t('Modal title'),
  description: t('Optional subtitle'),
  content: MyForm,
  footer: MyFormFooter,
  props: { /* forwarded to MyForm and MyFormFooter */ },
  size: 'md' // 'xs' | 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg' | 'full'
}).open();
```

Modals are rendered globally in `+layout.svelte` from `app.modals`. Plugins use `app.modal.create` with the same shape (`contentHost: 'plugin'` is set automatically).

## Modal content vs footer

- **`content`** — body only (fields, lists, inline row actions). No `Dialog.Root`, overlay, or title markup. Title and description come from `createModal`.
- **`footer`** — sticky bar for modal-level actions (Delete / Clear / Cancel / Close / Save / Create). Do **not** put those buttons in `content`.
- Close with `app.modal.get(modalId)?.close()` (or `getApp().modals.get(modalId)?.close()` in the app layer). Pass `modalId` (or a form model that closes) via `props`.
- Share state between content and footer via `props` (often a small reactive `.svelte.ts` model class).

Follow existing examples: `ActionForm` + `ActionFormFooter`, `QuoteForm` + `QuoteFormFooter`, `CollectionEditorForm` + `CollectionEditorFormFooter`.

## When not to use app modals

- **Confirmations** → `app.confirm.ask()` (`ConfirmDialog` in layout).
- **Small popovers/tooltips** → `@stream-kit/ui/popover` or `tooltip` attachment.
- **Plugin zip content** → declarative page blocks; built-in npm plugins may use `app.modal.create` via plugin API.
